<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need a Lot of Books! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need a Lot of Books!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Extract main ideas from informational texts. reading_2: Infer meaning from context clues in short texts. phonemic_awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can extract main ideas from informational texts about library procedures (e.g., 'Librarians help us find books.'). Can infer meaning from context clues in library-related texts (e.g., 'It's busy in the library, so we speak quietly.'). Can demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by identifying short and long vowel sounds in orally stated single-syllable words (e.g., recognizing /ɪ/ in 'pick' and /aɪ/ in 'find').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different types of books (fiction, non-fiction) and a `library` card. Students discuss in pairs what they remember about `library` activities from Lesson 1. The teacher asks: "What did we learn about libraries last week?" "What can you `find` in a `library`?" (Sociolinguistic: Use library-related vocabulary in peer discussions.)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a short sentence on the board: "You `should` be `quiet` in the `library`." The teacher reads the sentence clearly, modeling pronunciation. Then, the teacher demonstrates how to identify vowel sounds by saying "pick" and "find" and asking students to repeat and feel the difference. The teacher writes "/ɪ/" under "pick" and "/aɪ/" under "find." (Pronunciation: identifying short and long vowel sounds).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we shout in the `library`?" (Expected answer: No, you `should` be `quiet`.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound do you hear in 'pick'? Short /ɪ/ or long /aɪ/?" (Expected answer: Short /ɪ/.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "Our School Library Rules" or "How to `Borrow` a `Book`." It describes basic `library` procedures and rules. The text includes key vocabulary such as `librarian`, `catalog`, `checkout`, `return`, `read`, `quiet`, `helpful`, `interesting`, `many`, `should`, `can`, and `imperatives` like "Go to the `catalog`."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and interpret short written texts related to daily routines and simple instructions within a familiar context like the `library`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the main idea of this text?" (Expected answer: It's about how to use the `library` / `library` rules.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences based on the text and mark them True (T) or False (F). For example: "You `can borrow many books`." (T) "You `should` talk loudly in the `library`." (F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match short phrases from the text to simple pictures representing `librarian` help, `book checkout`, or being `quiet`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Extracting main ideas and inferring meaning from informational texts; Grammar: Past simple tense, modals `can/should`, `imperatives`; Vocabulary: `library`, `book`, `catalog`, `librarian`, `checkout`, `return`, `read`, `find`, `select`, `quiet`, `helpful`, `interesting`, `many`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the "Our School Library Rules" text. They fill in the blanks using words from a provided word bank (e.g., "A `librarian` `can` `help` you `find` a `book`." "Last week, I `read` an `interesting` `book`."). This reinforces vocabulary and grammar in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Vowel Sound Identification):</b> The teacher writes a list of target words from the text (e.g., `pick`, `find`, `read`, `book`, `quiet`). Students identify if each word has a short or long vowel sound, writing 'S' for short or 'L' for long next to each word. The teacher models a few examples first (e.g., `pick` /ɪ/ (S), `find` /aɪ/ (L)).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Main Idea Match):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two short paragraphs related to library scenarios (e.g., one about `checkout` procedures, another about `research` using the `catalog`). They read each paragraph and then match it to a sentence that states its main idea, chosen from a list of options. They infer meaning from context clues to make the correct match.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Library Detective Reading Challenge." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a "Library Information Board" – a larger text or a collection of several small informational cards about different `library` sections, resources, or procedures (e.g., "How to `search` for a `book`", "Our `fiction` `section`", "Rules for `borrowing` `many` `books`").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Groups `read` the information on their board. Then, they receive a set of "Detective Questions" that require them to `extract main ideas` and `infer meaning` from the texts. For example: "What did you `read` about the `non-fiction` `section`?" "If you `wanted` to `borrow` `many` `books` last week, what `should` you `do` first?" "What does `available` mean in the sentence 'All `books` are `available` for `checkout`'?" They collaborate to `find` the answers and write them down. (Pragmatic: Listing information collaboratively; Asking for and giving information about resources).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in `reading` for information, collaborative interaction, and accurate `extraction` of main ideas and `inference` of meaning to complete the task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Library Detective Reading Challenge" for group collaboration and reading comprehension, followed by individual quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually complete a short "Library Check-Up" quiz. The quiz presents a new, short informational text (e.g., "Using the Digital `Catalog`") and asks 3-4 multiple-choice questions or short-answer questions that require them to `extract main ideas` (e.g., "What is the best way to `find` a `book` according to the text?") and `infer meaning` from context (e.g., "What does 'digital' mean in this text?"). It also includes one question to identify a short/long vowel sound in a key vocabulary word from the text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying the `main idea` (80% correct).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to `infer meaning` from `context clues` (75% correct).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of short and long vowel sounds (80% correct).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of `library-related vocabulary` in answers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I `read` about..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I `can` now `understand` `main ideas` in `texts` about..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `was` the most `interesting` thing you `read` about the `library` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What `did` you `find` easy or difficult about `reading` and `understanding` the texts?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading activities `worked` well, and students `were` able to `extract main ideas` from the short informational texts. The `phonemic awareness` practice `was helpful` for differentiating vowel sounds. Some students `needed` more time to `infer meaning` from longer sentences; I `should` provide more targeted practice with simpler `context clues` next time. I `will` also `review` the use of `past simple tense` for describing `library` visits `last week` before Lesson 3.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3, students `will` use the `vocabulary` and `grammar` from this reading lesson to `practice speaking` and `interacting` in `library` scenarios, such as `asking` and `giving information` about `books` and `making polite requests` to the `librarian`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>